IX. THE GLOBAL CORE AND LOCAL CURRICULUM

The Nahouri system distinguishes between educational standardization and cultural standardization.

Global Core

The global core establishes common standards for:

* mathematics
* science
* computing
* languages
* world history
* economics
* critical thinking
* digital literacy
* graduation requirements

Local Curriculum

Individual countries and communities retain authority over:

* national history
* indigenous languages
* local literature
* religion
* local geography
* cultural traditions
* national law
* local arts
* national civics

The objective is:

Educational interoperability without cultural homogenization.

⸻

X. STANDARDIZED SCHOOL CALENDAR

Primary and secondary schools would generally provide approximately 180–200 instructional days per year.

Countries could adapt the calendar to:

* climate
* agriculture
* national holidays
* religious holidays
* cultural traditions
* local circumstances

provided that minimum international instructional requirements are satisfied.

⸻

